  What a pair of counts is made of, and the two coordinate systems you can
  describe it in. Interactive, with every knob linked to every other.
bibliography: ../references.bib
link-citations: true
---

```{r}
#| label: setup
#| include: false
# This article is PRECOMPILED (see vignettes/precompile.R) because it fits a
# model. Figures are therefore generated once, here, and committed to
# vignettes/articles/figure/ alongside the .Rmd -- pkgdown does not re-run the
# chunks, so the images must already exist. svg keeps them scalable and
# text-diffable rather than binary.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment  = "#>",
  dev      = "svg",
  fig.path = "figure/"
)
```

Every joint family in this package — `bipois()` and `binegbin()`, each with a
`_partialobs()` constructor for data whose first count is sometimes missing — is built
by **trivariate reduction**. @holgate1964 and @karlis2003 use the construction
with Poisson components; @kirkpatrickApplyingMultivariateDiscrete2016 and
@kirkpatrickRMKdiscreteSundryDiscrete2022 with negative-binomial components. Three
independent counts are drawn, and one of the three enters both observed counts:

$$
y_1 = N_{\text{shared}} + N_1
\qquad
y_2 = N_{\text{shared}} + N_2
$$

The shared term induces the correlation between the pair. It is never observed, and is
marginalised out analytically in the likelihood. Everything else is the part
each source saw alone.

The widget below decomposes a pair, exposing **two coordinate systems at once,
each driving the other**.

## Why two coordinate systems

The dpars a family actually takes are three rates: `mu` for the shared
component, `lambdaone` and `lambdatwo` for the two excesses. That is what the
likelihood takes, but it is awkward to reason in: raise the overall level of
counting and all three move together, so no single one of them answers "how
much was there", "how much did the two sources agree", or "which source ran
high".

Those three questions have their own coordinates:

| | | |
|---|---|---|
| $M$ | overall level | $\mu + (\lambda_1 + \lambda_2)/2$ |
| $f$ | congruence, the share of $M$ both sources saw | $\mu / M$ |
| $\beta$ | source bias, bounded on $[-1,1]$ | $(\lambda_1 - \lambda_2)/(\lambda_1 + \lambda_2)$ |

The map between them is a bijection, so neither set is more "real": they are
two descriptions of one object. Drag anything below and watch the other five
respond.

```{=html}
<style>
  .pca { --pca-shared:#8A8072; --pca-one:#2D6A7F; --pca-two:#C4622D;
         --pca-ink:#3A3A3A; --pca-shared-d:#6B5D4F; --pca-rule:#999;
         --pca-panel:rgba(127,127,127,.06); --pca-edge:rgba(127,127,127,.35);
         font-size:14px; }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .pca { --pca-shared:#B3A692; --pca-one:#5FA8C4; --pca-two:#E08A50;
           --pca-ink:#D8D4CE; --pca-shared-d:#C0B49E; --pca-rule:#777; }
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .pca {
    --pca-shared:#B3A692; --pca-one:#5FA8C4; --pca-two:#E08A50;
    --pca-ink:#D8D4CE; --pca-shared-d:#C0B49E; --pca-rule:#777;
  }
  :root[data-theme="light"] .pca {
    --pca-shared:#8A8072; --pca-one:#2D6A7F; --pca-two:#C4622D;
    --pca-ink:#3A3A3A; --pca-shared-d:#6B5D4F; --pca-rule:#999;
  }
  .pca-cols { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(320px,1fr));
              gap:1.4rem; margin:.6rem 0 1rem; }
  .pca-group h4 { margin:.1rem 0 .5rem; font-size:.95rem; letter-spacing:.02em; }
  .pca-group { border:1px solid var(--pca-edge); border-radius:7px; padding:.7rem .8rem; }
  .pca-dial { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:.45rem; margin:.3rem 0; }
  /* Widths in rem, not em: `em` on .val resolves against .val's own smaller
     font-size, which made the box's real width hard to predict. The range needs
     an explicit min-width or it loses the space war to the number field's
     intrinsic size. The slider is the thing being dragged, so it gets the
     remaining space. */
  .pca-dial .lab { flex:0 0 6rem; font-size:.86rem; }
  .pca-dial .lab sub { line-height:0; font-size:.75em; }
  .pca-dial input[type=range] { flex:1 1 auto; min-width:6rem; width:auto;
    margin:0; accent-color:#7A8288; }
  .pca-dial .val { flex:0 0 3.8rem; width:3.8rem; box-sizing:border-box;
    font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
    text-align:right; font-size:.83rem; color:var(--pca-ink);
    background:transparent; border:1px solid var(--pca-edge);
    border-radius:4px; padding:.05rem .25rem; font-family:inherit; }
  .pca-note { font-size:.8rem; opacity:.72; margin:.45rem 0 0; line-height:1.45; }
  .pca-bar { display:flex; gap:.7rem; align-items:center; flex-wrap:wrap; margin:.2rem 0 .7rem; }
